{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 2 (score 329.4, best so far): the 0.07 m look-ahead and 16 mm footprint raised oval finish rate from 0.05 to 0.65 and s-bend progress from 0.15 to 0.45, so sensing lag was a real limit. Max error on every development track still sits at about 0.06 to 0.064 m, the off-track limit. Line-loss events remain at about 1 per run on s-bend and slalom. Iteration 1 showed that widening the spread alone (to ±0.036 m with 12 mm sensors) did not help. Geometry check: with a 16 mm footprint at ±0.036 m, the outermost sensor stops seeing tape at about 0.0565 m offset. That is just inside the 0.06 m off-track limit, so the robot can leave the line while the outer sensors are already blind. I therefore widen the array to ±0.050 m with seven sensors at a 16.7 mm pitch. The outer sensors then keep seeing tape to about 0.0705 m, which is past the off-track limit, so the controller always has a valid signal before the run ends. The seven sensors keep the same 16 mm footprint and y=0.07 m look-ahead that improved iteration 2. The drivetrain (track 0.10 m, radius 0.035 m, mass 0.6 kg) is unchanged, so this iteration isolates the sensor-array change. Body width stays at 0.12 m because the off-track limit is 0.06 m for any body width up to 0.18 m. Expected result: fewer line-loss events (toward 0.5 or lower per run) and lower max_error on s-bend and slalom, with the s-bend and slalom finish rates rising above zero. Risk: seven sensors with a wider spread may increase steering oscillation, which the Integrator should check.",
