  "intent": "Widen the sensor array from five sensors at ±0.036 m to seven sensors at ±0.050 m, keeping the 16 mm footprint and the 0.07 m look-ahead from iteration 2. The outer sensors then keep tape in view past the 0.06 m off-track limit, so the controller does not go blind before the robot leaves the track. The drivetrain stays as in iteration 2 so the sensor change is the only variable. Expected: lower line-loss events and max_error on s-bend and slalom, and higher finish rates there.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "controller_errors", "noise_robustness"]
  },
  "learning": "Hypotheses to test, one at a time where possible: (1) Iteration 2's look-ahead gain was real, so the outer-sensor blind zone was the next limit. Seven sensors at ±0.050 m should cut line-loss events versus iteration 2 (about 1.0 per run on s-bend and slalom). (2) If line losses drop but steering_oscillation rises sharply, the extra sensors are adding noise to the weighted signal, and the Integrator should use the outer weights sparingly. (3) If max_error still reaches 0.06 m with few line losses, the limit is actuator lag, so the next change is a lower mass (0.4 to 0.5 kg) or a smaller wheel_track for quicker turns. (4) If progress on the oval falls below iteration 2's 0.94, the wider array has cost speed and the spread should come back to ±0.042 m."
